The standard library already has a function for this exact rearrangement. It changes `nums` in place and also handles the descending case by wrapping back to the smallest order.

Dry run for `[1, 2, 3]`: the library changes it to `[1, 3, 2]`. For `[3, 2, 1]`, it changes it to `[1, 2, 3]`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void nextPermutation(vector<int>& nums) {
        next_permutation(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on the usual implementation.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The input vector is modified directly.

This keeps the usual three small operations separate: locate the pivot, swap it with the first larger value from the right, then reverse the tail. Looking from the right is important because that tail is in descending order.

For `[1, 3, 2]`, the pivot is `1`. Swapping with `2` gives `[2, 3, 1]`, and reversing the tail produces `[2, 1, 3]`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    nextPermutation(nums) {
        let pivot = nums.length - 2;

        while (pivot >= 0 && nums[pivot] >= nums[pivot + 1]) {
            pivot--;
        }

        if (pivot >= 0) {
            let right = nums.length - 1;
            while (nums[right] <= nums[pivot]) {
                right--;
            }

            [nums[pivot], nums[right]] = [nums[right], nums[pivot]];
        }

        let left = pivot + 1;
        let right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            [nums[left], nums[right]] = [nums[right], nums[left]];
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

When the array is already descending, `pivot` becomes `-1`, so the final loop reverses the full array. Duplicate values work because the successor search uses a strict greater-than check.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The method changes `nums` directly and returns nothing.

The useful part here is that the suffix after the pivot is already in descending order. Once the pivot is swapped with the right successor, reversing that suffix puts it in the smallest possible order.

If the scan finds no pivot, the complete slice is descending, so reversing the whole slice gives the wraparound result.

```go
func nextPermutation(nums []int) {
	pivot := len(nums) - 2

	for pivot >= 0 && nums[pivot] >= nums[pivot+1] {
		pivot--
	}

	if pivot >= 0 {
		successor := len(nums) - 1
		for nums[successor] <= nums[pivot] {
			successor--
		}
		nums[pivot], nums[successor] = nums[successor], nums[pivot]
	}

	left, right := pivot+1, len(nums)-1
	for left < right {
		nums[left], nums[right] = nums[right], nums[left]
		left++
		right--
	}
}
```

The function does not return a slice. The supplied slice is updated in place. Empty or single-element input is also safe because the loops do not perform any invalid access.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

The right side of the array becomes non-increasing when scanned from the end. I find the first place where the order can be increased, swap in a larger value, and then sort the remaining suffix.

This is a little slower than reversing the suffix, but the steps are quite direct. Here is a dry run for `[1, 2, 5, 4, 3]`: the pivot is `2`, it swaps with `3`, and sorting the suffix gives `[1, 3, 2, 4, 5]`.

```java
class Solution {
    public void nextPermutation(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int pivot = n - 2;

        while (pivot >= 0 && nums[pivot] >= nums[pivot + 1]) {
            pivot--;
        }

        if (pivot == -1) {
            sortRange(nums, 0, n - 1);
            return;
        }

        int successor = n - 1;
        while (nums[successor] <= nums[pivot]) {
            successor--;
        }

        swap(nums, pivot, successor);
        sortRange(nums, pivot + 1, n - 1);
    }

    private void sortRange(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        for (int i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
            int value = nums[i];
            int j = i - 1;

            while (j >= left && nums[j] > value) {
                nums[j + 1] = nums[j];
                j--;
            }
            nums[j + 1] = value;
        }
    }

    private void swap(int[] nums, int first, int second) {
        int temp = nums[first];
        nums[first] = nums[second];
        nums[second] = temp;
    }
}
```

A one-element array simply keeps its value because no pivot exists and sorting a one-item range changes nothing.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because insertion sort is used on the suffix.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

