## Intuition
The next permutation is the smallest rearrangement that is lexicographically greater than the current one. The key observation is: from the right end, the sequence is non-increasing until we hit a dip — the first index where nums[i] < nums[i+1]. That's our pivot. We swap it with the smallest element to its right that is still greater than it, then reverse the suffix to get the smallest possible arrangement.

## Approach
1.Traverse from the right to find the pivot — the first index i where nums[i] < nums[i+1].
2.If no pivot found, the array is the last permutation — reverse the whole array to get the first.
3.From the right, find the first element greater than nums[i] and swap them.
4.Reverse the suffix starting at i+1 to make it the smallest possible order.
## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    void nextPermutation(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int pivot = -1;

        // Step 1: Find the pivot (first dip from the right)
        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (nums[i] < nums[i + 1]) {
                pivot = i;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Step 2: If no pivot, reverse entire array (last permutation)
        if (pivot == -1) {
            reverse(nums.begin(), nums.end());
            return;
        }

        // Step 3: Find rightmost element greater than pivot and swap
        for (int i = n - 1; i > pivot; i--) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[pivot]) {
                swap(nums[i], nums[pivot]);
                break;
            }
        }

        // Step 4: Reverse the suffix after pivot
        reverse(nums.begin() + pivot + 1, nums.end());
    }
};
```

